      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The reference class is ten consecutive Florida initial snapshot prints, September 2025-June 2026. Their mean is 23.3%, while the latest three-print mean is 22.0%. This base rate supports a persistence anchor at the recent plateau. The earlier higher rates provide evidence of variation without requiring a return to their levels."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Level: use the April-June plateau of 22.0%. Momentum: apply zero adjustment because the latest two monthly changes are zero; do not extrapolate the older downward drift indefinitely. One-off effects: March's fall to 20% was partly reversed in April, so add no separate rebound adjustment. Policy mechanism: CMS identifies data matching and classification of renewals completed after forms are sent as influences on reported ex parte outcomes; the fetched evidence supplies no quantified Florida change warranting a policy adjustment."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: use a zero-drift persistence model with the ten fetched initial snapshot rates [27,24,24,24,24,24,20,22,22,22]. Level 22.0 plus momentum 0.0, one-off 0.0 and policy 0.0 gives 22.0%. Successive changes are [-3,0,0,0,0,-4,2,0,0], with mean -0.5556 and squared deviations summing to 26.2222. sigma = 1.8105 percentage points, computed as sqrt(26.2222/8). The one-month 80% half-width is 1.28*sigma = 2.3174 points. August is two monthly steps beyond June, so use sqrt(2) horizon scaling and add uniform whole-point rounding variance of 1/12: half-width = 1.28*sqrt(2*1.8105^2 + 1/12) = 3.2981 points. Implied bounds are 18.7019% and 25.2981%, rounded to 18.7% and 25.3%."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Publication-date audit: the official CMS snapshot release list reports June 2026 released September 25, 2026, but supplies no prospective August 2026 publication date. December 15, 2026 could not be verified from an official release calendar or announcement in this run. The specifications distinguish original reports due on the eighth from updated reports due on the fifteenth; submission deadlines do not establish publication dates. The registered resolutionDate is retained, with this verification requirement unmet."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The upside risk is an August cohort with more beneficiaries matched automatically, or an expansion in successful administrative matching; a rate above 25.3% would land outside the interval. The downside risk is a matching-system disruption, more renewals remaining pending, or increased classification as form-based renewals; a rate below 18.7% would land outside the interval. A substantial process or reporting change would invalidate the persistence assumption."
